648:regress\artifacts\adjudication20260909\codex_audit_20260908.err:662:tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31258:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  **FOUND BY GROK** on the `sgirandompixel' design panel, as a residual it explicitly said\nto file rather than grow the round with: *'`default:' of the COLOUR-mode switch still does\n`buf[n] = random()' and then writes those bytes.  Same class, different arm.'*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING before filing**: four `random()' calls at dev_sgi_re.c, anchor `buf[0] = random();',\nin the colour-mode `default:'.  `sgirandompixel' removed the ONE in `horrible_getputpixel()'; these four\nremain and are the reason `grep -c 'random()'` on that file is still non-zero after the fix.\n\nNobody else on that panel saw it -- not codex, not the packet-fed seats, not me -- and I had read the file\nseveral times that evening.  The design is presumably the same member 3 (decline + latched announce), but\nthe observable differs: this arm fills a BUFFER rather than a single caller variable, so what a declined\nfill should leave is its own question and the detector shape is not simply copied.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nSEVERITY RAISED MED -> HIGH, on a fact DERIVED rather than assumed.  The row said the fix\nwas *presumably* the same as [[sgirandompixel]]'s and that the observable differed.  **The reachability is now\nestablished and it is the SAME documented-mode structure, not merely the same shape.**\n\nMeasured at HEAD, three commands:\n  * the PUT colour switch implements exactly FOUR cases -- `DE_MODE_TYPE_' CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR -- and its\n    `default:' is `buf[0..3] = random()';\n  * `crmfbreg.h' documents **FIVE** colour types; the fifth is `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB  0x000000f0';\n  * `grep -c YCRCB src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **0**.  It is handled nowhere.\n\nAnd it is reached by an ordinary register write: `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK', mask `0xf0', so a\nguest selecting YCRCB lands on the random arm directly.\n\n*** SO THIS IS `#504's OWN SENTENCE AT A SECOND SITE: four of five DOCUMENTED modes work and the fifth\nreturns random data. ***  `#504` was graded HIGH on exactly that warrant, and this arm is arguably worse in\none respect -- `sgirandompixel' put a random value in `*color' for the CALLER to store, whereas this writes\nfour random bytes into the tile through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)' with no intermediary.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and the severity should be read with it: NO WITNESS.**  Nothing here shows a guest\ndriver selecting YCRCB -- only that the model accepts it and answers with random data.  The rung-3 attempt\nlooks cheap for the same reason `#504's did (`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this\ncode path; the colour type is a different field of the same mode word), and it is OWED before this ships.\n\n**The GET side does NOT share the defect** -- its `default:' reads the raw 32-bit value rather than inventing\none, so this is a PUT-only arm.  That asymmetry is itself worth carrying into the design: the two default\narms of one function already disagree about what an unhandled colour type means.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT defect from [[sgirebufuninit]] at the same lines: that one is about bytes NOBODY\nassigns; this is about bytes assigned from `random()'.  A fix for either leaves the other standing.\n\n*** READY TO RUN AS THE NEXT ROUND.  Three things established 2026-09-07 while `#505` worked in the\nsame function, none of which this row had. ***\n\n**1. IT IS A DIFFERENT SWITCH FROM THE ONE `#504` FIXED, and the row should say so plainly** -- I nearly\nrecorded this as the `#499` symmetric-cell shape and it is not.  `#504` de-fanged the **addressing-mode**\ndefault (`default: fatal(\"unimplemented dst_mode %i ...\"); *color = random();`).  This row is the\n**colour-mode** default of a different `switch` in the same function:\n\n    default:buf[0] = random();  buf[1] = random();\n            buf[2] = random();  buf[3] = random();\n            // TODO.\n            fatal(\"[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\\n\", color_mode);\n    }\n    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS|PHYSICAL);\n\n**There is no `break` and no decline: control falls straight into the write**, so four bytes of host\n`random()` land in guest memory at a guest-chosen tile offset.  Same family as `#504`, same function,\ndifferent arm -- *related by idiom, not by the one-cell-repair pattern.*\n\n**2. FULLY GUEST-REACHABLE, established by reading rather than assumed.**  `color_mode = mode &\nDE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`, and `mode` is the caller's `src_mode`/`dst_mode`, read straight out of `de_reg[]`\nfrom `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC`/`CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` with **no filtering anywhere on the path**.  The four\nimplemented types are 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30; `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` is **0xf0 and is documented in\n`crmfbreg.h`** but unimplemented, so a guest selecting a real, named mode reaches this arm.\n\n**3. A WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT AWAY.**  `#505` built `regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`,\na rung-3 probe that drives an XFER copy on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32` with both ladder controls and a\nsibling accept row, and whose `setup()` now takes **both** src and dst modes.  Pointing its `dst_mode` at\n`LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_32 | 0xf0` puts YCRCB into the PUT switch and reaches this arm.\n\n  *** THE ORACLE IS THE HARD PART AND IT NEEDS SAYING BEFORE THE ROUND STARTS: THE DEFECT'S OUTPUT IS\n  RANDOM, SO NO VALUE ROW CAN PIN IT. ***  `#504` hit exactly this and its answer is the precedent --\n  assert that the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value, i.e. that the write was DECLINED, rather\n  than trying to assert anything about what was written.  A determinism oracle is refuted in advance by\n  this project's own record: two seats showed on `#504` that stack reuse can make a wrong value\n  deterministic, so *\"the same value twice\"* proves nothing.\n\n**FIX SHAPE: the option set has a member already chosen for its sibling.**  `#504` used DECLINE + LATCHED\nANNOUNCE with its own bit (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10`), for the reason `#498` measured -- the four\nhandlers share one struct, so a shared latch is silenced by the other site's ordinary traffic.  **This arm\nneeds its OWN bit, `1 << 11`**, and `#504`'s comment already says the enum ends there on purpose so the\nnext maintainer finds it.  *That is a transferred in-tree warrant, not a \"cannot hurt\" addition.*\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG AND THE\nDISPOSITION SURVIVED. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE is justified, with an unconditional return from the PUT\ndefault.  But the brief misstates #504's oracle and the write's byte count; depth 8 also exposes\nuninitialized buffer bytes.\"*\n\n*** CORRECTION 1 -- THE ORACLE.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE COST A ROUND. ***  My brief said\n`#504`'s precedent asserts *the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value*.  It does not.  The seat\nopened the probe: `lin_b is fg` expects **`FG_VAL = 0x77665544`**, not the pre-paint `0x11111111`,\nbecause a declined source GET **preserves the caller's foreground value**, which the following PUT\nthen writes.  In the seat's words, *\"the probe explicitly rejects preserving the destination as a\ndifferent disposition.\"*\n\n  **So `#504` warrants the latch-and-return IDIOM and does NOT supply the unchanged-destination\n  ORACLE I attributed to it.**  Those are different transfers and I ran them together.\n\n  **THE ORACLE THE SEAT PROPOSES INSTEAD, and it is stronger than either:** observe **ZERO\n  `MEM_WRITE` CALLS** from an unsupported PUT, *alongside* unchanged destination bytes -- because\n  that pair **distinguishes \"no write\" from \"same final value\"**, which neither assertion does\n  alone.  It also notes that if removing the RNG use is contractual, that must be observed\n  SEPARATELY.\n\n  *And a sentinel value will not rescue a value oracle here:* each assignment converts a SEPARATE\n  `random()` result to `uint8_t`, so the whole-word improbability argument `#504`'s old witness used\n  does not establish an impossible byte pattern.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2 -- THE BYTE COUNT, and it is the SAME WIDTH-AXIS LESSON `#505` PAID FOR HOURS\nEARLIER. ***  My brief said *four* bytes of `random()` are written.  The write is `bufdepth` bytes,\nnot four: at bufdepth 8 the arm fills `buf[0..3]` with RNG and ships **four further UNINITIALISED\nstack bytes** with them; at bufdepth 1 or 2 fewer than four RNG bytes land.  **\"Four\" is true only\nat bufdepth 4** -- the width every example happens to use.  *That is the exact shape of `#505`'s\nclause-(d) floor breach, arriving in the next round's brief the same day, written by the person who\nhad just repaired it.*  Overlaps [[sgirebufuninit]]; the round must say which defect owns which\nbytes.\n\n**OPTION SET: CONFIRMED COMPLETE, and the grep found three in-tree alternatives worth naming:**\n  * the helper's own GET default already provides a raw ABGR-shaped fallback (`dev_sgi_re.c:358`);\n  * **PCC2 has a real ACCEPT precedent** (`dev_pcc2.c:427`) -- a read served from backing storage\n    before the default diagnostic;\n  * **SGI's ROP diagnostics latch BY VALUE** (`dev_sgi_re.c:850`) rather than one bit per site.\n\nNone establishes correct YCRCB PUT semantics.  *\"Mirroring the GET fallback would repeat the\nunsupported-authority problem you warned about.  The YCRCB name establishes an encoding; it does not\nestablish packing, conversion, or depth semantics.\"*  IMPLEMENT is defensible in principle but no\npermitted source supplies an implementation or a demonstrated driver selection.  **No additional\nwarranted disposition outside the option set** -- which is the option-set rule returning a clean\nnegative for once, and that is worth recording as such.\n\n**THE LATCH BIT IS CONFIRMED AVAILABLE AND THE OBJECTION TO ONE BIT IS RECORDED:** occupied bits\nenumerate as 0..10, `warned` is `uint32_t`, so **`1 << 11` is free**.  The strongest objection is\ndiagnostic coverage -- *an earlier unnamed mode suppresses a later YCRCB announcement* -- and\nper-mode latching has in-tree precedent (the ROP site above).  One bit stays consistent with this\nhelper's existing policy, so it is a judgement the round must make explicitly rather than inherit.\n\n**FIX SHAPE (Q2), in the seat's words:** *a terminal default arm containing a conditional\nannouncement followed by an **unconditional function return***.  Note this is the SKETCH RULE's\nterritory -- the PUT branch's write sits after the switch and is shared by all five arms, so the\nreturn must be checked against the whole enclosing function before it is written.\n\n*** AND A WARNING THAT SAVES THE ROUND A FALSE START: `#504`'s PROBE CANNOT BE REUSED UNCHANGED. ***\n*\"#504's `lin_b is fg` remains green under edits confined to this colour default.  Both of that\nprobe's mode constants use RGBA, so it never selects the PUT colour default.  Its setup cannot be\ntransferred unchanged.\"*  `#505`'s probe is the better base -- its `setup()` already takes both src\nand dst modes -- but the same check applies to it and must be run, not assumed.\n\n**ENUMERATION CONFIRMED, with a qualification I did not have:** the twelve default selections are\neleven unnamed (`0x40`..`0xe0`) plus the named `0xf0` YCRCB, and they reach the arm *\"when the\npreceding coordinate/addressing/tile-validity checks pass\"*.\n\n*** THE ORACLE QUESTION, ANSWERED BEFORE THE ROUND STARTED -- AND THE NAIVE IMPLEMENTATION WOULD\nHAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN.  codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`.\n***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Your distinction is conditionally sound.  A correctly scoped write-attempt observer\nworks for Round A; **the existing stepped probe supplies none.**  This fork's debugger watchpoints\noffer a qualified alternative.\"*\n\n**MY DISTINCTION SURVIVES, with a precision I had not stated.**  A count separates *no request* from\n*a request carrying the existing bytes* -- but *\"zero calls does not establish correct rejection\nunless the unsupported arm was ACTUALLY REACHED: an earlier return or an operation that never ran\nalso produces zero.\"*  That is the attribution problem, and it means the round owes a reach control\nbeside the count.\n\n*** THE MECHANISM EXISTS: debugger WRITE WATCHPOINTS, checked in the common memory path BEFORE\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch, and neither PHYSICAL nor NO_EXCEPTIONS excludes this helper's write.  A\npty driver can observe them with NO instrumented build. ***  (`memory_rw.c:273`.)\n\n*** AND IT HAS TWO DECISIVE LIMITS, THE FIRST OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN: ***\n  1. **The check requires `!single_step`, and the existing probe's `run_ops()` uses `step`.**  So\n     *\"merely adding watchpoints and counting their messages to that execution scheme could report\n     ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"*  The row would have reported \"no write happened\" while writes happened.\n     **A watchpoint oracle therefore requires the FREE-RUNNING probe shape, not the stepped one.**\n  2. It also requires `!about_to_enter_single_step` and **SETS that flag after its first report**, so\n     later pixel writes inside the same drawing command go unreported.  What Python would count is\n     *printed watchpoint reports*, not `MEM_WRITE` calls -- watchpoints carry no hit count.\n\n**SO THE ACHIEVABLE CLAIM IS BOUNDED, and the round must say so rather than overclaim:** the\nmechanism supports an **any-attempt-to-this-range** witness when observation is active throughout the\nrelevant execution.  It does NOT support *\"exactly N calls\"* or *\"no writes anywhere from this\nhelper\"*.  The conclusion must read **\"no observed write attempt to this range under these validated\nconditions\"** -- watching the destination cannot exclude a write to an UNINTENDED address, which is\nexactly the failure mode [[sgirebufuninit]] and the `#505` tiled row both hit in other forms.\n\n**THE SHIPPABLE DESIGN, in the seat's own construction:** verified arm reach and completed execution,\nan active observer that DOES detect a supported write to the same range (the liveness half), then no\nmatching write report for the rejected operation, with unchanged verified destination bytes as\ncorroboration and the announce count testing \"announce once\" separately.  **That is four rows, not\none**, and knowing it now is worth more than discovering it after the fix.\n\n*This is the carrier's `!single_step` family arriving through a NEW mechanism.  The carrier records\nthat a stepped probe cannot observe INTERRUPT DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`'s\n\"do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\").  The WATCHPOINT case is the same guard on a\ndifferent feature and was not written down.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: REVISE -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE **with a `return` before the PUT transfer**.  It\nrefuted TWO of the brief's premises, and one of them was the brief's central design claim. ***\n\n**S3 WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE PREMISE THE WHOLE 'COPY THE SIBLING' READING RESTED ON.**  I wrote that\n`sgirandompixel` fixed *the GET side of this same helper*.  It did not: **it repaired the\nADDRESSING-MODE default at `:235`, which sits BEFORE GET and PUT diverge and returns for both\ndirections.  The COLOUR GET default at `:358` still performs a raw conversion.**  Two different\narms -- so the sibling is a precedent for the DISPOSITION and not for the site, and its comment\ncannot simply be copied.\n\n**S5 WAS WRONG, AND THIS ONE UNBLOCKS THE ROUND.**  I claimed the decline's 'what does `buf` hold'\nquestion was the SAME question as [[sgirebufuninit]]'s, making that row a prerequisite.  codex:\n*\"A declined PUT need not leave anything defined in `buf`, provided nothing consumes it.  The helper\nitself owns the memory write.  Its callers receive no buffer to transfer afterward.\"*\n\n    remove the fill, fall through   -> transfers indeterminate stack bytes   REJECT\n    zero the span, fall through     -> overwrites destination with zero      needs a warrant\n    RETURN from the PUT default     -> preserves destination bytes           PREFERRED\n\n> **So the two rows are INDEPENDENT and there is no patch ordering.**  This round decides whether an\n> unsupported PUT writes anything at all; `sgirebufuninit` decides how paths that DO transfer avoid\n> consuming uninitialised bytes.  *Initialising the buffer would neither remove the four `random()`\n> calls nor justify writing zeros for an unsupported format.*\n\n**S1 needs a width qualification:** four bytes are ASSIGNED randomly, but the transfer length is 1, 2,\n4 or 8.  A narrow transfer exposes only the prefix; an eight-byte transfer additionally consumes four\nuninitialised bytes.\n\n**S2's inference was a CORRELATE and the real population is twelve, not one.**  `grep -c YCRCB` = 0\nproves only that one NAMED constant is absent.  Enumerating `mode & 0xf0`: sixteen values, **four\nexplicit PUT cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR) and TWELVE reaching `default:`** -- eleven unnamed plus YCRCB.\nThe DE register handler stores the mode without filtering the type nibble, and both line and rectangle\ncallers pass it through unchanged.  *The named-constant search was the same error that cost the\n`asicbadaddr` census a 4x undercount.*\n\n**FAULT IS AVAILABLE HERE and my brief implied otherwise** -- `memory_rw.c:603` delivers MIPS\nexceptions.  codex rejects it anyway on better grounds than availability: the helper returns `void`,\nits transfers request `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and the outer device handler returns success, so FAULT would\nneed an error-propagation design and a warrant for the guest-visible exception.\n\n**THE LATCH MUST BE AN INDEPENDENT BIT, NOT `SGIRE_W_BADMODE`** -- sharing it would let an ADDRESSING\nfailure suppress the COLOUR diagnostic.  That is `#490`'s measured latch-collision class, predicted\nhere before the code exists.\n\n**Q4, the predicted escape:** condition the new `return` on `bufdepth == 4` -- the canonical clause-(d)\nshape, conditioning the repair on a field the transfer consumes.  **codex expects `rgb tiled64` to stay\ngreen under it**, because that row varies the GET SOURCE's addressing and width while keeping a linear\nfour-byte RGBA destination: *\"its name does not establish PUT coverage.\"*  The proposed closure is the\ndestination axes together -- twelve unsupported types x four widths x four address modes = **192\ncombinations** -- with whole-span checks including eight-byte spans.  *Source-based prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**Q3, the witness:** source inspection supports a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on stock IP32 and codex\nfound no machine-description blocker.  But **the sibling probe's green rows are NOT a witness for this\nrow**: its setup fixes the DESTINATION to RGBA, so varying its source-mode argument never exercises the\ncolour PUT default.  Record **'source-reachable; rung-3 attempt pending'** -- codex ran nothing, as\ninstructed, so the attempted-probe prerequisite is NOT yet discharged.\n\n*** A CONSEQUENCE OF THE DECLINE DESIGN, found by the codex seat on the SIBLING brief\n(`sgimtedepth4dead`) and cross-filed here because it is this row's design that it bears on. ***\n\n*\"A helper return before accessing the buffer leaves the caller's colour unchanged.  MTE still\nperforms the dependent PUT, and its `bg` persists across iterations -- so a declined GET can reuse an\nearlier source value.\"*\n\n**That is distinct from buffer initialisation and it bites the agreed repair.**  The design settled\nfor this row is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with a `return` before the transfer -- and on the MTE\npath the caller's `bg` is a loop-scope variable, so a declined pixel does not leave the destination\nuntouched: **it writes the PREVIOUS pixel's colour.**  A decline that silently smears the last good\nvalue across a run of unsupported pixels is a different wrong answer from `random()`, and arguably a\nmore plausible-looking one.\n\n*The round owes an answer: does the decline return before the PUT as well, and if so what does the\ndestination hold?  This was not visible from this row's own brief, which framed the question around\n`buf` rather than around the caller's colour variable.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 2, 2026-09-07: I CONFLATED A DECLINED GET WITH A DECLINED PUT.  PASS 1's DESIGN\nSTANDS UNCHANGED. ***\n\nThe PUT-default `return` exits **before** `memory_rw(... MEM_WRITE ...)`, so whatever the caller's\n`color` holds, that declined PUT does not transfer it.  Leaving the destination's existing bytes\nintact is the honest disposition for an unsupported write.  **The `bg` observation is about the\ndependent-PUT problem, which is a different row.**\n\n**AND MY S4 DID NOT ESTABLISH REACHABILITY:** MTE builds modes from addressing and depth bits only,\nleaving the colour type at CI, and its one colour override sits behind the unreachable `depth == 4`.\n**Neither construction reaches this PUT colour default**, so the MTE `bg` behaviour does not bear on\nthis row at all.\n\n*Two further qualifications on my wording: MTE reloads `bg` from `CRIME_MTE_BG` on **each transfer\ninvocation**, so a declined GET retains the latest value from THAT invocation or the register's\ninitial value -- never a previous command's local `bg`.  And \"the whole rectangle deterministically\nreceives the last good colour\" is too strong independently, because CI's accepted PUT initialises\nonly `buf[0]` and wider depths consume uninitialised bytes.*\n\n**THE LATCH GRANULARITY IS CONFIRMED AS APPROPRIATE AND ITS LIMIT STATED:** one message per device\ninstance for the latch's lifetime -- so a thousand declined pixels produce one line, **and none if an\nearlier command already set the bit** (the struct is zeroed at init; there is no per-command reset).\nIt deliberately cannot distinguish one declined pixel from a rectangle; reporting extent would need\ncounting or a command summary.\n\n**RESIDUAL FOUND: bounding the PUT warning does not bound every warning from the operation.**  The\n**GET colour default still logs without a latch**, and DE can perform that GET for a\ndestination-dependent raster operation before ever reaching the declined PUT.  So a round that\nlatches only the PUT arm leaves a guest-drivable unlatched diagnostic in the same code path.\n\n**THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT:** change the new `return;` to `break;`, keeping the latch and the\nannouncement.  Execution resumes at the memory write and consumes the uninitialised buffer -- **it\nbreaks the no-write contract without restoring `random()`**.  `rgb samemode` and `rgb tiled64` both\nexpected to stay green (supported RGB/RGBA, never reaching the default).  *The coverage gap is wider\nthan MTE: the scoped scripts never select an unsupported destination colour type at all, so their\ngreen results cannot establish this decline.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS PROBLEM IS CHARACTERISED, AND IT IS HARDER THAN THE ROW ASSUMED.  From the codex\nORACLE answer (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`), read in full\n2026-09-07 -- most of this had been sitting unread. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS FIRST, and the second changes the FIX, not just the witness:**\n\n1. *\"The buffer lacks a declaration initializer; it is not NEVER INITIALIZED.  PUT arms assign\n   prefixes, and GET fills the requested transfer length.\"*  The row's shorthand was sloppy.\n2. *** \"THE MISSING `break` IS NOT WHAT PERMITS THE WRITE.  The write is AFTER the switch, so adding\n   `break` would still reach it.  **The proposed return matters.**\" ***  A `break`-shaped repair\n   would look right and do nothing -- exactly the sketch-versus-enclosing-control-flow rule this\n   project minted after four readers proposed a `return` into a function with a common tail.\n\n*** THE OBSERVER EXISTS, AND IT IS DISQUALIFIED BY THE PROBE'S OWN EXECUTION MODE. ***\n\nThe fork has debugger WRITE-WATCHPOINTS, and the common memory path checks them **before**\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch -- *\"neither `PHYSICAL` nor `NO_EXCEPTIONS` excludes this helper's\nwrite\"* (`memory_rw.c:273`).  A matching attempt prints `WATCHPOINT: write`, which a pty driver can\ncount with no instrumented build.  **But two conditions kill it as written:**\n\n    memory_rw.c:273   the check requires  !single_step\n                      -- and sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's run_ops() uses `step'.  Adding\n                      watchpoints to that scheme \"could report ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"\n\n    same check        also requires  !about_to_enter_single_step, and SETS that flag after its\n                      first report -- so subsequent pixel writes inside the SAME drawing command\n                      go unreported.\n\n> **This is the carrier's single-stepping rule arriving on a new mechanism.**  It already records\n> that a stepped probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`: *\"Do not cause\n> interrupts while single-stepping\"*).  **Watchpoints are a second capability gated the same way**,\n> and the same discriminator applies: ask what the observation depends on before designing around\n> it.\n\n**WHAT THE MECHANISM CAN AND CANNOT SUPPORT, in codex's words:** it supports an\n**any-attempt-to-this-range** witness *\"when observation is active throughout the relevant\nexecution\"* -- i.e. FREE-RUNNING, not stepped -- and even then only the first report survives the\nflag.  It *\"cannot automatically support 'exactly N calls', or 'no writes anywhere from this\nhelper'.  A literal, attributed call count needs a suitable host-side observer or `harness:`\nwitness.\"*\n\n*** AND Q3 DEMOLISHES THE CHEAP ORACLE: \"unchanged destination bytes prove only unchanged OBSERVED\nCONTENTS.\" ***  Equality at the observation points is also consistent with a same-value write, a\nwrite followed by restoration, a write to somewhere else, no drawing operation at all, or an earlier\nhelper return.  **Two of those are live here rather than theoretical**: the helper returns on an\ninvalid tile pointer (`dev_sgi_re.c:274`), and *\"the memory path can discard an out-of-RAM write\nwhile returning success; corresponding reads can return zeros\"* (`memory_rw.c:662`).\n\n> **So a destination-equality row needs a control establishing that the pre-paint REALLY EXISTS and\n> that a SUPPORTED operation through the same path actually lands** -- otherwise \"unchanged\" is\n> indistinguishable from \"nothing ever happened\", which is the vacuity shape in its purest form.\n\n**THE TWO VIABLE PATHS, neither cheap:**\n* **(a) a FREE-RUNNING probe with a write-watchpoint** -- any-attempt semantics, first report only,\n  and it must abandon the stepped `run_ops()` idiom the sibling probes use;\n* **(b) a `harness:` witness with a host-side observer** -- an attributed call count, which the\n  ladder admits only when the round's subject IS the apparatus, so it would need care not to become\n  the rung-1 shortcut the ladder exists to refuse.\n\n**STATUS: this row is NOT held for fable5 and is the only HIGH row that is not** -- but its blocker\nis now a characterised capability gap rather than an open question, and neither path is a session's\nwork.  *Recorded in full because most of this analysis had been sitting unread in the artifact since\nit was produced, which is this session's most-repeated failure.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  FIVE SEATS, UNANIMOUS ON BOTH QUESTIONS -- and the strongest answer\nREFUSED TO CERTIFY ITSELF, which is the part worth keeping. ***\n\n**THE DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, implemented as candidate (b) -- skip the\nPUT's `memory_rw` for a declined mode.  Never an early `return`.**\n\n*** grok's REFRAMING IS BETTER THAN MY OPTION LIST AND IT DISSOLVES THE QUESTION THE ROW WAS FILED\nON. ***  The row asked *\"what should a declined BUFFER FILL leave behind?\"*, treating `buf` as the\nanalogue of `#504`'s `*color`.  It is not:\n\n> *\"Destination of a PUT is guest memory at `tileptr + ofs`, not `buf`.  `#504` declined the\n> helper's OUTPUT.  The PUT output is the store.  Skipping the store leaves whatever the guest\n> already had -- deterministic, no invented colour, no host bytes.  `buf` can stay uninitialised\n> because it is not sent.\"*\n\nSo the fix is not a fill policy at all.  **The question \"what should the buffer contain\" was the\nwrong question**, and my option (a) (zero-fill) was demoted to a fallback for exactly one case:\nif some tail consumes `buf` or assumes the store happened.\n\n**AND IT CORRECTED MY (a) IN PASSING:** zero-filling only `buf[0..3]` to mirror the `random()` shape\nis NOT (a) -- `bufdepth` can be 8, so that form *\"is this arm already instantiating\n`sgirebufuninit`\"*.  If (a) is ever taken it must define the whole payload.\n\n*** IT DECLINED TO CERTIFY (b), NAMING EXACTLY THE GAP: *** *\"That tail is not in the packet.  I\nwill not guess `x++`, a dirty bit, a return value, or a use of `buf`.  I cannot certify (b) against\n'nine lines down.'\"*  That is this project's own sketch rule, applied by a seat to its own\nrecommendation, unprompted beyond the caution in the brief.\n\n## *** BOTH OF ITS UNCHECKABLE PREMISES ARE NOW SETTLED BY ME, BY OPENING THE FILE ***\n\n**1. THERE IS NO COMMON TAIL.**  `horrible_getputpixel()` is `void`, and after the PUT store the\n`else` runs the GET path and the function ENDS -- `}` closing the else, `}` closing the function,\nnothing between them.  No dirty bit, no counter, no return value, no later use of `buf`.\n**So (b) is safe**, and the `put_ok` flag form grok gave is belt-and-braces rather than necessary --\nwhich is still the right way to write it, because it cannot become the early-`return` trap under a\nlater edit.\n\n**2. `fatal()` DOES NOT EXIT.**  `src/core/debugmsg.c`: `va_start; va_debug; va_end;` and return --\nno `exit()`, no early-out.  grok flagged that if `fatal()` aborted, *\"the random fill is dead and\nthe guest-visible row collapses.\"*  It does not abort, so **the random bytes really do reach\n`memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)` and the defect stands.**\n\n*That premise should have been IN the packet.  The carrier records `fatal()`'s non-exit as a\nstanding fact and I did not inline it -- the settled-premises rule exists precisely so a packet-fed\nseat need not park on something already known.*\n\n## ROUND SCOPE: TWO ROUNDS, 5 of 5, each with its own reason\n\nNot one round with `sgirebufuninit`, despite the two sitting in the same `switch`.  **minimax gave\nthe sharpest form:** *\"a regression in the CI/depth-8 path and a regression in the YCRCB path\nproduce the same test failure\"* -- i.e. one oracle cannot tell them apart, which is exactly the\nround-scope rule's test rather than a preference.  grok: *\"They sit in the same `switch`.  That is\nnot the test.\"*  **And it named the trap explicitly: zero-initialising `buf` at its declaration\nwould silently convert this round's (b) into (a) and smuggle `sgirebufuninit`'s fix in with it.**\n\n*This was asked as an open question arguing both ways, so the unanimity is worth something -- unlike\nunanimity on a brief-supplied FACT, which this project has measured to be worth nothing.*\n\n## RESIDUALS RAISED, NOT YET DISPOSED\n\n* grok: the arm *\"is already confused -- it announces unimplemented (`TODO` + `fatal`) and then\n  IMPLEMENTS with noise.  The announce is the right instinct (unlatched, per-pixel, therefore wrong\n  shape).  The fill is the defect.\"*  So the existing `fatal()` becomes the latched announce; it is\n  not added, it is fixed.\n* **12 of 16 mode values reach `default:` and only `0xf0` is documented.**  grok accepted the\n  arithmetic (`16 - 4`, needing no header) but declined the reachability claim as *\"claims about\n  other code, not in the packet\"* -- correctly.  **A round taking this owes the reachability\n  check**, and `IMPLEMENT` (member 7) for YCRCB is a separate design: *\"no YCbCr packing,\n  subsample, or byte order in this packet; 11 of the 12 values are not even named.\"*\n* Q4/Q5 (detector and witness) are in the seat answers and NOT yet consolidated here -- that is an\n  UNREAD-REVIEW debt on this row until it is.\n\n*** THE Q4/Q5 UNREAD-REVIEW DEBT IS DISCHARGED, AND IT WAS WORTH PAYING: THE PANEL'S OWN MAJORITY\nDETECTOR WOULD HAVE SHIPPED AN ESCAPABLE ROW. ***\n\nI recorded the detector and witness answers as unread.  Reading all five:\n\n## THE DETECTOR -- convergent, and it is a DESTINATION CANARY, not \"looks non-random\"\n\nPre-fill the target bytes with a distinctive non-zero canary, execute ONE PUT with a declined mode,\nread back, and **pass iff the canary is bit-identical** -- i.e. the oracle is *the store did not\nhappen*, not *the value is not random*.  A liveness control in the SAME script repeats with a\ndefined mode (RGBA or CI) and the canary MUST change; without it a dead PUT path greens the round.\n\n*** THE HOLE, and three of the five seats were in it: DO NOT PIN THE TEST TO `0xf0` ALONE. ***\ndeepseek and kimi both proposed YCRCB `0xf0` as *the* test mode, kimi arguing it is the value a real\nguest would plausibly select.  grok:\n\n> *\"A mutant `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random would escape.  Use at\n> least one UNDOCUMENTED value (`0x40` is the first hole) as the primary; `0xf0` can be a second\n> vector, not the only one.\"*\n\n**That is a real escape against the majority design.**  The documented mode is the most tempting\nchoice and the weakest one, because a mutant can special-case exactly it.\n\n**FOUR MORE STRENGTHENINGS, one per seat, none of them duplicated:**\n* **grok** -- vary `bufdepth` over at least {1, 4, 8}, so a mutant that skips the store only when\n  `bufdepth == 4` dies.\n* **minimax** -- make the oracle **CONJUNCTIVE**: the latch transitioned 0 -> 1 *during that pixel*\n  (record the PRE-state) AND `memory_rw` was not called for it.  Either conjunct alone is escapable,\n  and recording the pre-state defeats a mutant that pre-sets the latch at init.\n* **kimi** -- assert the announce count is **exactly 1**, which is what actually pins the latch.\n* **glm** -- assert the ADJACENT bytes are unchanged too, since the write is only `bufdepth` bytes\n  at `ofs`; and run twice asserting identical transcripts.\n\n**AND grok'S HONEST LIMIT ON ITS OWN ORACLE:** keeping `buf[i] = random()` while setting\n`put_ok = 0` leaves the canary intact, so a destination-only oracle cannot see the leftover\n`random()` calls.  It calls that *\"a successful (b), not an escape, PROVIDED the patch also deletes\nthe four `random()` calls -- same edit, not a second oracle\"*, and explicitly declines to make\n\"`random()` is not called\" the oracle because an LCG or `rand()` defeats it.\n\n## *** THE BLOCKING PREMISE THREE SEATS RAISED INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW SETTLED -- BY ME, IN TWO READS,\n## AND IT SETTLES IN FAVOUR OF THE ROUND. ***\n\nglm, kimi and deepseek each named the SAME single fact as the one that decides whether a witness\nexists at all: **does the mode-register write path mask or reject `0xf0` (and the eleven\nundocumented values) BEFORE they reach this helper?**  glm put the consequence sharply: if it does,\n*\"no witness exists, the round is documented-only, and '12 of 16' then describes host-reachable\nstates no real guest can reach.\"*\n\n**THERE IS NO VALIDATION.**  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`:\n\n    :1098  /*  Treat all registers as read/write, by default. ... */\n    :1112  d->de_reg[regnr] = idata;              <- unconditional, unmasked\n\nand the `switch (relative_addr)` below it reaches `case CRIME_DE_MODE_DST:` only to `debug()` the\nvalue and `break` -- no mask, no rejection, no range test.  `draw_primitive()` then reads\n`d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000)/4]` straight back, and `horrible_getputpixel()` computes\n`color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`.\n\n> **So a guest store of ANY 32-bit value to CRIME_DE_MODE_DST is kept verbatim, and bits 4-7 select\n> among all 16 colour modes -- twelve of which reach the `random()` arm.  The witness is\n> constructible and the round is NOT documented-only.**\n\n*The file's own comment is the authority here, which is the strongest form: it is not that I failed\nto find a validator, it is that the code states it treats these registers as raw read/write.*\n\n**THE REMAINING UNSETTLED ITEM is smaller than it looked:** kimi asked whether `#504`'s witness\nitself selected a declined mode.  That is a question about a shipped probe, answerable by reading\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py`, and it is no longer load-bearing now that the register path\nis settled.",
670:regress\artifacts\sgirandomcolour\SCOPE_brief.md:58:			fatal("[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\n", color_mode);
680:regress\artifacts\sgirandomcolour\PASS2_brief_20260910.md:227:(scratchpad/patch_sgirc.py, 58 changed lines): SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR = 1 << 12 as the last enumerator, an `ok'
684:regress\artifacts\sgirandomcolour\killtable_20260910\PRETABLE.out:4:(scratchpad/patch_sgirc.py, 58 changed lines): SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR = 1 << 12 as the last enumerator, an `ok'
695:regress\artifacts\sgirandomcolour\codex_witness_20260908.err:2717:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31269:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  **FOUND BY GROK** on the `sgirandompixel' design panel, as a residual it explicitly said\nto file rather than grow the round with: *'`default:' of the COLOUR-mode switch still does\n`buf[n] = random()' and then writes those bytes.  Same class, different arm.'*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING before filing**: four `random()' calls at dev_sgi_re.c, anchor `buf[0] = random();',\nin the colour-mode `default:'.  `sgirandompixel' removed the ONE in `horrible_getputpixel()'; these four\nremain and are the reason `grep -c 'random()'` on that file is still non-zero after the fix.\n\nNobody else on that panel saw it -- not codex, not the packet-fed seats, not me -- and I had read the file\nseveral times that evening.  The design is presumably the same member 3 (decline + latched announce), but\nthe observable differs: this arm fills a BUFFER rather than a single caller variable, so what a declined\nfill should leave is its own question and the detector shape is not simply copied.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nSEVERITY RAISED MED -> HIGH, on a fact DERIVED rather than assumed.  The row said the fix\nwas *presumably* the same as [[sgirandompixel]]'s and that the observable differed.  **The reachability is now\nestablished and it is the SAME documented-mode structure, not merely the same shape.**\n\nMeasured at HEAD, three commands:\n  * the PUT colour switch implements exactly FOUR cases -- `DE_MODE_TYPE_' CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR -- and its\n    `default:' is `buf[0..3] = random()';\n  * `crmfbreg.h' documents **FIVE** colour types; the fifth is `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB  0x000000f0';\n  * `grep -c YCRCB src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **0**.  It is handled nowhere.\n\nAnd it is reached by an ordinary register write: `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK', mask `0xf0', so a\nguest selecting YCRCB lands on the random arm directly.\n\n*** SO THIS IS `#504's OWN SENTENCE AT A SECOND SITE: four of five DOCUMENTED modes work and the fifth\nreturns random data. ***  `#504` was graded HIGH on exactly that warrant, and this arm is arguably worse in\none respect -- `sgirandompixel' put a random value in `*color' for the CALLER to store, whereas this writes\nfour random bytes into the tile through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)' with no intermediary.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and the severity should be read with it: NO WITNESS.**  Nothing here shows a guest\ndriver selecting YCRCB -- only that the model accepts it and answers with random data.  The rung-3 attempt\nlooks cheap for the same reason `#504's did (`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this\ncode path; the colour type is a different field of the same mode word), and it is OWED before this ships.\n\n**The GET side does NOT share the defect** -- its `default:' reads the raw 32-bit value rather than inventing\none, so this is a PUT-only arm.  That asymmetry is itself worth carrying into the design: the two default\narms of one function already disagree about what an unhandled colour type means.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT defect from [[sgirebufuninit]] at the same lines: that one is about bytes NOBODY\nassigns; this is about bytes assigned from `random()'.  A fix for either leaves the other standing.\n\n*** READY TO RUN AS THE NEXT ROUND.  Three things established 2026-09-07 while `#505` worked in the\nsame function, none of which this row had. ***\n\n**1. IT IS A DIFFERENT SWITCH FROM THE ONE `#504` FIXED, and the row should say so plainly** -- I nearly\nrecorded this as the `#499` symmetric-cell shape and it is not.  `#504` de-fanged the **addressing-mode**\ndefault (`default: fatal(\"unimplemented dst_mode %i ...\"); *color = random();`).  This row is the\n**colour-mode** default of a different `switch` in the same function:\n\n    default:buf[0] = random();  buf[1] = random();\n            buf[2] = random();  buf[3] = random();\n            // TODO.\n            fatal(\"[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\\n\", color_mode);\n    }\n    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS|PHYSICAL);\n\n**There is no `break` and no decline: control falls straight into the write**, so four bytes of host\n`random()` land in guest memory at a guest-chosen tile offset.  Same family as `#504`, same function,\ndifferent arm -- *related by idiom, not by the one-cell-repair pattern.*\n\n**2. FULLY GUEST-REACHABLE, established by reading rather than assumed.**  `color_mode = mode &\nDE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`, and `mode` is the caller's `src_mode`/`dst_mode`, read straight out of `de_reg[]`\nfrom `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC`/`CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` with **no filtering anywhere on the path**.  The four\nimplemented types are 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30; `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` is **0xf0 and is documented in\n`crmfbreg.h`** but unimplemented, so a guest selecting a real, named mode reaches this arm.\n\n**3. A WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT AWAY.**  `#505` built `regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`,\na rung-3 probe that drives an XFER copy on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32` with both ladder controls and a\nsibling accept row, and whose `setup()` now takes **both** src and dst modes.  Pointing its `dst_mode` at\n`LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_32 | 0xf0` puts YCRCB into the PUT switch and reaches this arm.\n\n  *** THE ORACLE IS THE HARD PART AND IT NEEDS SAYING BEFORE THE ROUND STARTS: THE DEFECT'S OUTPUT IS\n  RANDOM, SO NO VALUE ROW CAN PIN IT. ***  `#504` hit exactly this and its answer is the precedent --\n  assert that the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value, i.e. that the write was DECLINED, rather\n  than trying to assert anything about what was written.  A determinism oracle is refuted in advance by\n  this project's own record: two seats showed on `#504` that stack reuse can make a wrong value\n  deterministic, so *\"the same value twice\"* proves nothing.\n\n**FIX SHAPE: the option set has a member already chosen for its sibling.**  `#504` used DECLINE + LATCHED\nANNOUNCE with its own bit (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10`), for the reason `#498` measured -- the four\nhandlers share one struct, so a shared latch is silenced by the other site's ordinary traffic.  **This arm\nneeds its OWN bit, `1 << 11`**, and `#504`'s comment already says the enum ends there on purpose so the\nnext maintainer finds it.  *That is a transferred in-tree warrant, not a \"cannot hurt\" addition.*\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG AND THE\nDISPOSITION SURVIVED. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE is justified, with an unconditional return from the PUT\ndefault.  But the brief misstates #504's oracle and the write's byte count; depth 8 also exposes\nuninitialized buffer bytes.\"*\n\n*** CORRECTION 1 -- THE ORACLE.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE COST A ROUND. ***  My brief said\n`#504`'s precedent asserts *the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value*.  It does not.  The seat\nopened the probe: `lin_b is fg` expects **`FG_VAL = 0x77665544`**, not the pre-paint `0x11111111`,\nbecause a declined source GET **preserves the caller's foreground value**, which the following PUT\nthen writes.  In the seat's words, *\"the probe explicitly rejects preserving the destination as a\ndifferent disposition.\"*\n\n  **So `#504` warrants the latch-and-return IDIOM and does NOT supply the unchanged-destination\n  ORACLE I attributed to it.**  Those are different transfers and I ran them together.\n\n  **THE ORACLE THE SEAT PROPOSES INSTEAD, and it is stronger than either:** observe **ZERO\n  `MEM_WRITE` CALLS** from an unsupported PUT, *alongside* unchanged destination bytes -- because\n  that pair **distinguishes \"no write\" from \"same final value\"**, which neither assertion does\n  alone.  It also notes that if removing the RNG use is contractual, that must be observed\n  SEPARATELY.\n\n  *And a sentinel value will not rescue a value oracle here:* each assignment converts a SEPARATE\n  `random()` result to `uint8_t`, so the whole-word improbability argument `#504`'s old witness used\n  does not establish an impossible byte pattern.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2 -- THE BYTE COUNT, and it is the SAME WIDTH-AXIS LESSON `#505` PAID FOR HOURS\nEARLIER. ***  My brief said *four* bytes of `random()` are written.  The write is `bufdepth` bytes,\nnot four: at bufdepth 8 the arm fills `buf[0..3]` with RNG and ships **four further UNINITIALISED\nstack bytes** with them; at bufdepth 1 or 2 fewer than four RNG bytes land.  **\"Four\" is true only\nat bufdepth 4** -- the width every example happens to use.  *That is the exact shape of `#505`'s\nclause-(d) floor breach, arriving in the next round's brief the same day, written by the person who\nhad just repaired it.*  Overlaps [[sgirebufuninit]]; the round must say which defect owns which\nbytes.\n\n**OPTION SET: CONFIRMED COMPLETE, and the grep found three in-tree alternatives worth naming:**\n  * the helper's own GET default already provides a raw ABGR-shaped fallback (`dev_sgi_re.c:358`);\n  * **PCC2 has a real ACCEPT precedent** (`dev_pcc2.c:427`) -- a read served from backing storage\n    before the default diagnostic;\n  * **SGI's ROP diagnostics latch BY VALUE** (`dev_sgi_re.c:850`) rather than one bit per site.\n\nNone establishes correct YCRCB PUT semantics.  *\"Mirroring the GET fallback would repeat the\nunsupported-authority problem you warned about.  The YCRCB name establishes an encoding; it does not\nestablish packing, conversion, or depth semantics.\"*  IMPLEMENT is defensible in principle but no\npermitted source supplies an implementation or a demonstrated driver selection.  **No additional\nwarranted disposition outside the option set** -- which is the option-set rule returning a clean\nnegative for once, and that is worth recording as such.\n\n**THE LATCH BIT IS CONFIRMED AVAILABLE AND THE OBJECTION TO ONE BIT IS RECORDED:** occupied bits\nenumerate as 0..10, `warned` is `uint32_t`, so **`1 << 11` is free**.  The strongest objection is\ndiagnostic coverage -- *an earlier unnamed mode suppresses a later YCRCB announcement* -- and\nper-mode latching has in-tree precedent (the ROP site above).  One bit stays consistent with this\nhelper's existing policy, so it is a judgement the round must make explicitly rather than inherit.\n\n**FIX SHAPE (Q2), in the seat's words:** *a terminal default arm containing a conditional\nannouncement followed by an **unconditional function return***.  Note this is the SKETCH RULE's\nterritory -- the PUT branch's write sits after the switch and is shared by all five arms, so the\nreturn must be checked against the whole enclosing function before it is written.\n\n*** AND A WARNING THAT SAVES THE ROUND A FALSE START: `#504`'s PROBE CANNOT BE REUSED UNCHANGED. ***\n*\"#504's `lin_b is fg` remains green under edits confined to this colour default.  Both of that\nprobe's mode constants use RGBA, so it never selects the PUT colour default.  Its setup cannot be\ntransferred unchanged.\"*  `#505`'s probe is the better base -- its `setup()` already takes both src\nand dst modes -- but the same check applies to it and must be run, not assumed.\n\n**ENUMERATION CONFIRMED, with a qualification I did not have:** the twelve default selections are\neleven unnamed (`0x40`..`0xe0`) plus the named `0xf0` YCRCB, and they reach the arm *\"when the\npreceding coordinate/addressing/tile-validity checks pass\"*.\n\n*** THE ORACLE QUESTION, ANSWERED BEFORE THE ROUND STARTED -- AND THE NAIVE IMPLEMENTATION WOULD\nHAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN.  codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`.\n***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Your distinction is conditionally sound.  A correctly scoped write-attempt observer\nworks for Round A; **the existing stepped probe supplies none.**  This fork's debugger watchpoints\noffer a qualified alternative.\"*\n\n**MY DISTINCTION SURVIVES, with a precision I had not stated.**  A count separates *no request* from\n*a request carrying the existing bytes* -- but *\"zero calls does not establish correct rejection\nunless the unsupported arm was ACTUALLY REACHED: an earlier return or an operation that never ran\nalso produces zero.\"*  That is the attribution problem, and it means the round owes a reach control\nbeside the count.\n\n*** THE MECHANISM EXISTS: debugger WRITE WATCHPOINTS, checked in the common memory path BEFORE\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch, and neither PHYSICAL nor NO_EXCEPTIONS excludes this helper's write.  A\npty driver can observe them with NO instrumented build. ***  (`memory_rw.c:273`.)\n\n*** AND IT HAS TWO DECISIVE LIMITS, THE FIRST OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN: ***\n  1. **The check requires `!single_step`, and the existing probe's `run_ops()` uses `step`.**  So\n     *\"merely adding watchpoints and counting their messages to that execution scheme could report\n     ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"*  The row would have reported \"no write happened\" while writes happened.\n     **A watchpoint oracle therefore requires the FREE-RUNNING probe shape, not the stepped one.**\n  2. It also requires `!about_to_enter_single_step` and **SETS that flag after its first report**, so\n     later pixel writes inside the same drawing command go unreported.  What Python would count is\n     *printed watchpoint reports*, not `MEM_WRITE` calls -- watchpoints carry no hit count.\n\n**SO THE ACHIEVABLE CLAIM IS BOUNDED, and the round must say so rather than overclaim:** the\nmechanism supports an **any-attempt-to-this-range** witness when observation is active throughout the\nrelevant execution.  It does NOT support *\"exactly N calls\"* or *\"no writes anywhere from this\nhelper\"*.  The conclusion must read **\"no observed write attempt to this range under these validated\nconditions\"** -- watching the destination cannot exclude a write to an UNINTENDED address, which is\nexactly the failure mode [[sgirebufuninit]] and the `#505` tiled row both hit in other forms.\n\n**THE SHIPPABLE DESIGN, in the seat's own construction:** verified arm reach and completed execution,\nan active observer that DOES detect a supported write to the same range (the liveness half), then no\nmatching write report for the rejected operation, with unchanged verified destination bytes as\ncorroboration and the announce count testing \"announce once\" separately.  **That is four rows, not\none**, and knowing it now is worth more than discovering it after the fix.\n\n*This is the carrier's `!single_step` family arriving through a NEW mechanism.  The carrier records\nthat a stepped probe cannot observe INTERRUPT DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`'s\n\"do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\").  The WATCHPOINT case is the same guard on a\ndifferent feature and was not written down.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: REVISE -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE **with a `return` before the PUT transfer**.  It\nrefuted TWO of the brief's premises, and one of them was the brief's central design claim. ***\n\n**S3 WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE PREMISE THE WHOLE 'COPY THE SIBLING' READING RESTED ON.**  I wrote that\n`sgirandompixel` fixed *the GET side of this same helper*.  It did not: **it repaired the\nADDRESSING-MODE default at `:235`, which sits BEFORE GET and PUT diverge and returns for both\ndirections.  The COLOUR GET default at `:358` still performs a raw conversion.**  Two different\narms -- so the sibling is a precedent for the DISPOSITION and not for the site, and its comment\ncannot simply be copied.\n\n**S5 WAS WRONG, AND THIS ONE UNBLOCKS THE ROUND.**  I claimed the decline's 'what does `buf` hold'\nquestion was the SAME question as [[sgirebufuninit]]'s, making that row a prerequisite.  codex:\n*\"A declined PUT need not leave anything defined in `buf`, provided nothing consumes it.  The helper\nitself owns the memory write.  Its callers receive no buffer to transfer afterward.\"*\n\n    remove the fill, fall through   -> transfers indeterminate stack bytes   REJECT\n    zero the span, fall through     -> overwrites destination with zero      needs a warrant\n    RETURN from the PUT default     -> preserves destination bytes           PREFERRED\n\n> **So the two rows are INDEPENDENT and there is no patch ordering.**  This round decides whether an\n> unsupported PUT writes anything at all; `sgirebufuninit` decides how paths that DO transfer avoid\n> consuming uninitialised bytes.  *Initialising the buffer would neither remove the four `random()`\n> calls nor justify writing zeros for an unsupported format.*\n\n**S1 needs a width qualification:** four bytes are ASSIGNED randomly, but the transfer length is 1, 2,\n4 or 8.  A narrow transfer exposes only the prefix; an eight-byte transfer additionally consumes four\nuninitialised bytes.\n\n**S2's inference was a CORRELATE and the real population is twelve, not one.**  `grep -c YCRCB` = 0\nproves only that one NAMED constant is absent.  Enumerating `mode & 0xf0`: sixteen values, **four\nexplicit PUT cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR) and TWELVE reaching `default:`** -- eleven unnamed plus YCRCB.\nThe DE register handler stores the mode without filtering the type nibble, and both line and rectangle\ncallers pass it through unchanged.  *The named-constant search was the same error that cost the\n`asicbadaddr` census a 4x undercount.*\n\n**FAULT IS AVAILABLE HERE and my brief implied otherwise** -- `memory_rw.c:603` delivers MIPS\nexceptions.  codex rejects it anyway on better grounds than availability: the helper returns `void`,\nits transfers request `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and the outer device handler returns success, so FAULT would\nneed an error-propagation design and a warrant for the guest-visible exception.\n\n**THE LATCH MUST BE AN INDEPENDENT BIT, NOT `SGIRE_W_BADMODE`** -- sharing it would let an ADDRESSING\nfailure suppress the COLOUR diagnostic.  That is `#490`'s measured latch-collision class, predicted\nhere before the code exists.\n\n**Q4, the predicted escape:** condition the new `return` on `bufdepth == 4` -- the canonical clause-(d)\nshape, conditioning the repair on a field the transfer consumes.  **codex expects `rgb tiled64` to stay\ngreen under it**, because that row varies the GET SOURCE's addressing and width while keeping a linear\nfour-byte RGBA destination: *\"its name does not establish PUT coverage.\"*  The proposed closure is the\ndestination axes together -- twelve unsupported types x four widths x four address modes = **192\ncombinations** -- with whole-span checks including eight-byte spans.  *Source-based prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**Q3, the witness:** source inspection supports a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on stock IP32 and codex\nfound no machine-description blocker.  But **the sibling probe's green rows are NOT a witness for this\nrow**: its setup fixes the DESTINATION to RGBA, so varying its source-mode argument never exercises the\ncolour PUT default.  Record **'source-reachable; rung-3 attempt pending'** -- codex ran nothing, as\ninstructed, so the attempted-probe prerequisite is NOT yet discharged.\n\n*** A CONSEQUENCE OF THE DECLINE DESIGN, found by the codex seat on the SIBLING brief\n(`sgimtedepth4dead`) and cross-filed here because it is this row's design that it bears on. ***\n\n*\"A helper return before accessing the buffer leaves the caller's colour unchanged.  MTE still\nperforms the dependent PUT, and its `bg` persists across iterations -- so a declined GET can reuse an\nearlier source value.\"*\n\n**That is distinct from buffer initialisation and it bites the agreed repair.**  The design settled\nfor this row is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with a `return` before the transfer -- and on the MTE\npath the caller's `bg` is a loop-scope variable, so a declined pixel does not leave the destination\nuntouched: **it writes the PREVIOUS pixel's colour.**  A decline that silently smears the last good\nvalue across a run of unsupported pixels is a different wrong answer from `random()`, and arguably a\nmore plausible-looking one.\n\n*The round owes an answer: does the decline return before the PUT as well, and if so what does the\ndestination hold?  This was not visible from this row's own brief, which framed the question around\n`buf` rather than around the caller's colour variable.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 2, 2026-09-07: I CONFLATED A DECLINED GET WITH A DECLINED PUT.  PASS 1's DESIGN\nSTANDS UNCHANGED. ***\n\nThe PUT-default `return` exits **before** `memory_rw(... MEM_WRITE ...)`, so whatever the caller's\n`color` holds, that declined PUT does not transfer it.  Leaving the destination's existing bytes\nintact is the honest disposition for an unsupported write.  **The `bg` observation is about the\ndependent-PUT problem, which is a different row.**\n\n**AND MY S4 DID NOT ESTABLISH REACHABILITY:** MTE builds modes from addressing and depth bits only,\nleaving the colour type at CI, and its one colour override sits behind the unreachable `depth == 4`.\n**Neither construction reaches this PUT colour default**, so the MTE `bg` behaviour does not bear on\nthis row at all.\n\n*Two further qualifications on my wording: MTE reloads `bg` from `CRIME_MTE_BG` on **each transfer\ninvocation**, so a declined GET retains the latest value from THAT invocation or the register's\ninitial value -- never a previous command's local `bg`.  And \"the whole rectangle deterministically\nreceives the last good colour\" is too strong independently, because CI's accepted PUT initialises\nonly `buf[0]` and wider depths consume uninitialised bytes.*\n\n**THE LATCH GRANULARITY IS CONFIRMED AS APPROPRIATE AND ITS LIMIT STATED:** one message per device\ninstance for the latch's lifetime -- so a thousand declined pixels produce one line, **and none if an\nearlier command already set the bit** (the struct is zeroed at init; there is no per-command reset).\nIt deliberately cannot distinguish one declined pixel from a rectangle; reporting extent would need\ncounting or a command summary.\n\n**RESIDUAL FOUND: bounding the PUT warning does not bound every warning from the operation.**  The\n**GET colour default still logs without a latch**, and DE can perform that GET for a\ndestination-dependent raster operation before ever reaching the declined PUT.  So a round that\nlatches only the PUT arm leaves a guest-drivable unlatched diagnostic in the same code path.\n\n**THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT:** change the new `return;` to `break;`, keeping the latch and the\nannouncement.  Execution resumes at the memory write and consumes the uninitialised buffer -- **it\nbreaks the no-write contract without restoring `random()`**.  `rgb samemode` and `rgb tiled64` both\nexpected to stay green (supported RGB/RGBA, never reaching the default).  *The coverage gap is wider\nthan MTE: the scoped scripts never select an unsupported destination colour type at all, so their\ngreen results cannot establish this decline.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS PROBLEM IS CHARACTERISED, AND IT IS HARDER THAN THE ROW ASSUMED.  From the codex\nORACLE answer (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`), read in full\n2026-09-07 -- most of this had been sitting unread. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS FIRST, and the second changes the FIX, not just the witness:**\n\n1. *\"The buffer lacks a declaration initializer; it is not NEVER INITIALIZED.  PUT arms assign\n   prefixes, and GET fills the requested transfer length.\"*  The row's shorthand was sloppy.\n2. *** \"THE MISSING `break` IS NOT WHAT PERMITS THE WRITE.  The write is AFTER the switch, so adding\n   `break` would still reach it.  **The proposed return matters.**\" ***  A `break`-shaped repair\n   would look right and do nothing -- exactly the sketch-versus-enclosing-control-flow rule this\n   project minted after four readers proposed a `return` into a function with a common tail.\n\n*** THE OBSERVER EXISTS, AND IT IS DISQUALIFIED BY THE PROBE'S OWN EXECUTION MODE. ***\n\nThe fork has debugger WRITE-WATCHPOINTS, and the common memory path checks them **before**\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch -- *\"neither `PHYSICAL` nor `NO_EXCEPTIONS` excludes this helper's\nwrite\"* (`memory_rw.c:273`).  A matching attempt prints `WATCHPOINT: write`, which a pty driver can\ncount with no instrumented build.  **But two conditions kill it as written:**\n\n    memory_rw.c:273   the check requires  !single_step\n                      -- and sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's run_ops() uses `step'.  Adding\n                      watchpoints to that scheme \"could report ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"\n\n    same check        also requires  !about_to_enter_single_step, and SETS that flag after its\n                      first report -- so subsequent pixel writes inside the SAME drawing command\n                      go unreported.\n\n> **This is the carrier's single-stepping rule arriving on a new mechanism.**  It already records\n> that a stepped probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`: *\"Do not cause\n> interrupts while single-stepping\"*).  **Watchpoints are a second capability gated the same way**,\n> and the same discriminator applies: ask what the observation depends on before designing around\n> it.\n\n**WHAT THE MECHANISM CAN AND CANNOT SUPPORT, in codex's words:** it supports an\n**any-attempt-to-this-range** witness *\"when observation is active throughout the relevant\nexecution\"* -- i.e. FREE-RUNNING, not stepped -- and even then only the first report survives the\nflag.  It *\"cannot automatically support 'exactly N calls', or 'no writes anywhere from this\nhelper'.  A literal, attributed call count needs a suitable host-side observer or `harness:`\nwitness.\"*\n\n*** AND Q3 DEMOLISHES THE CHEAP ORACLE: \"unchanged destination bytes prove only unchanged OBSERVED\nCONTENTS.\" ***  Equality at the observation points is also consistent with a same-value write, a\nwrite followed by restoration, a write to somewhere else, no drawing operation at all, or an earlier\nhelper return.  **Two of those are live here rather than theoretical**: the helper returns on an\ninvalid tile pointer (`dev_sgi_re.c:274`), and *\"the memory path can discard an out-of-RAM write\nwhile returning success; corresponding reads can return zeros\"* (`memory_rw.c:662`).\n\n> **So a destination-equality row needs a control establishing that the pre-paint REALLY EXISTS and\n> that a SUPPORTED operation through the same path actually lands** -- otherwise \"unchanged\" is\n> indistinguishable from \"nothing ever happened\", which is the vacuity shape in its purest form.\n\n**THE TWO VIABLE PATHS, neither cheap:**\n* **(a) a FREE-RUNNING probe with a write-watchpoint** -- any-attempt semantics, first report only,\n  and it must abandon the stepped `run_ops()` idiom the sibling probes use;\n* **(b) a `harness:` witness with a host-side observer** -- an attributed call count, which the\n  ladder admits only when the round's subject IS the apparatus, so it would need care not to become\n  the rung-1 shortcut the ladder exists to refuse.\n\n**STATUS: this row is NOT held for fable5 and is the only HIGH row that is not** -- but its blocker\nis now a characterised capability gap rather than an open question, and neither path is a session's\nwork.  *Recorded in full because most of this analysis had been sitting unread in the artifact since\nit was produced, which is this session's most-repeated failure.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  FIVE SEATS, UNANIMOUS ON BOTH QUESTIONS -- and the strongest answer\nREFUSED TO CERTIFY ITSELF, which is the part worth keeping. ***\n\n**THE DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, implemented as candidate (b) -- skip the\nPUT's `memory_rw` for a declined mode.  Never an early `return`.**\n\n*** grok's REFRAMING IS BETTER THAN MY OPTION LIST AND IT DISSOLVES THE QUESTION THE ROW WAS FILED\nON. ***  The row asked *\"what should a declined BUFFER FILL leave behind?\"*, treating `buf` as the\nanalogue of `#504`'s `*color`.  It is not:\n\n> *\"Destination of a PUT is guest memory at `tileptr + ofs`, not `buf`.  `#504` declined the\n> helper's OUTPUT.  The PUT output is the store.  Skipping the store leaves whatever the guest\n> already had -- deterministic, no invented colour, no host bytes.  `buf` can stay uninitialised\n> because it is not sent.\"*\n\nSo the fix is not a fill policy at all.  **The question \"what should the buffer contain\" was the\nwrong question**, and my option (a) (zero-fill) was demoted to a fallback for exactly one case:\nif some tail consumes `buf` or assumes the store happened.\n\n**AND IT CORRECTED MY (a) IN PASSING:** zero-filling only `buf[0..3]` to mirror the `random()` shape\nis NOT (a) -- `bufdepth` can be 8, so that form *\"is this arm already instantiating\n`sgirebufuninit`\"*.  If (a) is ever taken it must define the whole payload.\n\n*** IT DECLINED TO CERTIFY (b), NAMING EXACTLY THE GAP: *** *\"That tail is not in the packet.  I\nwill not guess `x++`, a dirty bit, a return value, or a use of `buf`.  I cannot certify (b) against\n'nine lines down.'\"*  That is this project's own sketch rule, applied by a seat to its own\nrecommendation, unprompted beyond the caution in the brief.\n\n## *** BOTH OF ITS UNCHECKABLE PREMISES ARE NOW SETTLED BY ME, BY OPENING THE FILE ***\n\n**1. THERE IS NO COMMON TAIL.**  `horrible_getputpixel()` is `void`, and after the PUT store the\n`else` runs the GET path and the function ENDS -- `}` closing the else, `}` closing the function,\nnothing between them.  No dirty bit, no counter, no return value, no later use of `buf`.\n**So (b) is safe**, and the `put_ok` flag form grok gave is belt-and-braces rather than necessary --\nwhich is still the right way to write it, because it cannot become the early-`return` trap under a\nlater edit.\n\n**2. `fatal()` DOES NOT EXIT.**  `src/core/debugmsg.c`: `va_start; va_debug; va_end;` and return --\nno `exit()`, no early-out.  grok flagged that if `fatal()` aborted, *\"the random fill is dead and\nthe guest-visible row collapses.\"*  It does not abort, so **the random bytes really do reach\n`memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)` and the defect stands.**\n\n*That premise should have been IN the packet.  The carrier records `fatal()`'s non-exit as a\nstanding fact and I did not inline it -- the settled-premises rule exists precisely so a packet-fed\nseat need not park on something already known.*\n\n## ROUND SCOPE: TWO ROUNDS, 5 of 5, each with its own reason\n\nNot one round with `sgirebufuninit`, despite the two sitting in the same `switch`.  **minimax gave\nthe sharpest form:** *\"a regression in the CI/depth-8 path and a regression in the YCRCB path\nproduce the same test failure\"* -- i.e. one oracle cannot tell them apart, which is exactly the\nround-scope rule's test rather than a preference.  grok: *\"They sit in the same `switch`.  That is\nnot the test.\"*  **And it named the trap explicitly: zero-initialising `buf` at its declaration\nwould silently convert this round's (b) into (a) and smuggle `sgirebufuninit`'s fix in with it.**\n\n*This was asked as an open question arguing both ways, so the unanimity is worth something -- unlike\nunanimity on a brief-supplied FACT, which this project has measured to be worth nothing.*\n\n## RESIDUALS RAISED, NOT YET DISPOSED\n\n* grok: the arm *\"is already confused -- it announces unimplemented (`TODO` + `fatal`) and then\n  IMPLEMENTS with noise.  The announce is the right instinct (unlatched, per-pixel, therefore wrong\n  shape).  The fill is the defect.\"*  So the existing `fatal()` becomes the latched announce; it is\n  not added, it is fixed.\n* **12 of 16 mode values reach `default:` and only `0xf0` is documented.**  grok accepted the\n  arithmetic (`16 - 4`, needing no header) but declined the reachability claim as *\"claims about\n  other code, not in the packet\"* -- correctly.  **A round taking this owes the reachability\n  check**, and `IMPLEMENT` (member 7) for YCRCB is a separate design: *\"no YCbCr packing,\n  subsample, or byte order in this packet; 11 of the 12 values are not even named.\"*\n* Q4/Q5 (detector and witness) are in the seat answers and NOT yet consolidated here -- that is an\n  UNREAD-REVIEW debt on this row until it is.\n\n*** THE Q4/Q5 UNREAD-REVIEW DEBT IS DISCHARGED, AND IT WAS WORTH PAYING: THE PANEL'S OWN MAJORITY\nDETECTOR WOULD HAVE SHIPPED AN ESCAPABLE ROW. ***\n\nI recorded the detector and witness answers as unread.  Reading all five:\n\n## THE DETECTOR -- convergent, and it is a DESTINATION CANARY, not \"looks non-random\"\n\nPre-fill the target bytes with a distinctive non-zero canary, execute ONE PUT with a declined mode,\nread back, and **pass iff the canary is bit-identical** -- i.e. the oracle is *the store did not\nhappen*, not *the value is not random*.  A liveness control in the SAME script repeats with a\ndefined mode (RGBA or CI) and the canary MUST change; without it a dead PUT path greens the round.\n\n*** THE HOLE, and three of the five seats were in it: DO NOT PIN THE TEST TO `0xf0` ALONE. ***\ndeepseek and kimi both proposed YCRCB `0xf0` as *the* test mode, kimi arguing it is the value a real\nguest would plausibly select.  grok:\n\n> *\"A mutant `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random would escape.  Use at\n> least one UNDOCUMENTED value (`0x40` is the first hole) as the primary; `0xf0` can be a second\n> vector, not the only one.\"*\n\n**That is a real escape against the majority design.**  The documented mode is the most tempting\nchoice and the weakest one, because a mutant can special-case exactly it.\n\n**FOUR MORE STRENGTHENINGS, one per seat, none of them duplicated:**\n* **grok** -- vary `bufdepth` over at least {1, 4, 8}, so a mutant that skips the store only when\n  `bufdepth == 4` dies.\n* **minimax** -- make the oracle **CONJUNCTIVE**: the latch transitioned 0 -> 1 *during that pixel*\n  (record the PRE-state) AND `memory_rw` was not called for it.  Either conjunct alone is escapable,\n  and recording the pre-state defeats a mutant that pre-sets the latch at init.\n* **kimi** -- assert the announce count is **exactly 1**, which is what actually pins the latch.\n* **glm** -- assert the ADJACENT bytes are unchanged too, since the write is only `bufdepth` bytes\n  at `ofs`; and run twice asserting identical transcripts.\n\n**AND grok'S HONEST LIMIT ON ITS OWN ORACLE:** keeping `buf[i] = random()` while setting\n`put_ok = 0` leaves the canary intact, so a destination-only oracle cannot see the leftover\n`random()` calls.  It calls that *\"a successful (b), not an escape, PROVIDED the patch also deletes\nthe four `random()` calls -- same edit, not a second oracle\"*, and explicitly declines to make\n\"`random()` is not called\" the oracle because an LCG or `rand()` defeats it.\n\n## *** THE BLOCKING PREMISE THREE SEATS RAISED INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW SETTLED -- BY ME, IN TWO READS,\n## AND IT SETTLES IN FAVOUR OF THE ROUND. ***\n\nglm, kimi and deepseek each named the SAME single fact as the one that decides whether a witness\nexists at all: **does the mode-register write path mask or reject `0xf0` (and the eleven\nundocumented values) BEFORE they reach this helper?**  glm put the consequence sharply: if it does,\n*\"no witness exists, the round is documented-only, and '12 of 16' then describes host-reachable\nstates no real guest can reach.\"*\n\n**THERE IS NO VALIDATION.**  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`:\n\n    :1098  /*  Treat all registers as read/write, by default. ... */\n    :1112  d->de_reg[regnr] = idata;              <- unconditional, unmasked\n\nand the `switch (relative_addr)` below it reaches `case CRIME_DE_MODE_DST:` only to `debug()` the\nvalue and `break` -- no mask, no rejection, no range test.  `draw_primitive()` then reads\n`d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000)/4]` straight back, and `horrible_getputpixel()` computes\n`color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`.\n\n> **So a guest store of ANY 32-bit value to CRIME_DE_MODE_DST is kept verbatim, and bits 4-7 select\n> among all 16 colour modes -- twelve of which reach the `random()` arm.  The witness is\n> constructible and the round is NOT documented-only.**\n\n*The file's own comment is the authority here, which is the strongest form: it is not that I failed\nto find a validator, it is that the code states it treats these registers as raw read/write.*\n\n**THE REMAINING UNSETTLED ITEM is smaller than it looked:** kimi asked whether `#504`'s witness\nitself selected a declined mode.  That is a question about a shipped probe, answerable by reading\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py`, and it is no longer load-bearing now that the register path\nis settled.",
717:regress\artifacts\sgirandomcolour\codex_analysis_20260908.err:461:			fatal("[ get: color mode = 0x%x ]\n", color_mode);
